# <LANGUAGE> rules

> Applies to: <PATH GLOBS, e.g. `src/**/*.py`, `services/api/**`>
> Toolchain version: <e.g. Python 3.12 / Node 20 / Go 1.22>  ·  Package manager: <e.g. uv / pnpm / go mod>

## Commands (run from repo root)

| Purpose        | Command                                   |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Install deps   | `<INSTALL CMD>`                           |
| Build          | `<BUILD CMD or "N/A">`                    |
| Run (dev)      | `<RUN CMD>`                                |
| Test (all)     | `<TEST CMD>`                              |
| Test (single)  | `<SINGLE-TEST CMD, incl. how to target one test>` |
| Lint           | `<LINT CMD>`                              |
| Format         | `<FORMAT CMD>`                            |
| Typecheck      | `<TYPECHECK CMD or "N/A">`               |

- The formatter above is **authoritative** — don't hand-format; run it.
- Before declaring done: run format, lint, typecheck, and tests (see root DoD).

## Style & structure

- Formatter/linter config lives in `<CONFIG FILE, e.g. pyproject.toml / .eslintrc / .golangci.yml>` — do not override inline unless the file says so.
- Naming: <CONVENTION, e.g. snake_case funcs, PascalCase types>.
- Module/file layout: <e.g. one public type per file; keep files < 300 LOC per root rule>.
- Public APIs get doc comments (<e.g. docstrings / JSDoc / godoc>); comments explain *why*, not *what*.

## Errors & logging

- Error handling idiom: <e.g. raise exceptions / return `error` / Result type>. Follows root "fail fast, no silent fallback" rule.
- Logging: use <LOGGER/LIB>, structured where possible; never `print`-debug in committed code; never log secrets or PII.

## Testing

- Framework: <e.g. pytest / vitest / go test>. Test files live in <LOCATION/PATTERN>.
- New code ships with tests. Don't weaken, skip, or `xfail`/`t.Skip` a test to make a suite pass.
- <UNIT vs INTEGRATION guidance; what must be covered>.

## Dependencies

- Add via `<ADD-DEP CMD>`; commit the lockfile (`<LOCKFILE>`).
- Pin/lock versions; prefer maintained, permissively licensed libraries.
- Per root rule: qualify a new dependency with the user before adding it.
- Do not hand-edit generated/vendored files: <e.g. `<LOCKFILE>`, `dist/`, `*_pb2.py`, migrations>.

## Language-specific gotchas

- <e.g. async/await rules, GIL, nullability, ownership, build tags — anything an agent gets wrong here>.
